## The Problem: Why We Need Requests and Limits

So first we are going to explore few challenges post which we will be discussing on how this specific feature allows us to overcome them.

### Challenge 1: Resource Starvation

Now the first challenge here states that a specific pod that is scheduled in a worker node can consume more resources than expected, which will affect all of the other pods that are part of the same node.

So let's assume that you have a pod, you have app B bot, and suddenly you also have a app C pod. But this pod is consuming a lot of Ram. It is consuming a lot of CPU as part of the worker node.

Now what the issue will happen is that since this worker node has a finite amount of memory and finite amount of CPU, if app C pod is consuming most of the Ram and CPU, then app A and app B pod will not really get enough resources and this will in turn affect the overall production environment.

So ideally this is something that you do not really want. And this is also one of the common things that you will see in production environments if the requests and limits are not really set.

### Challenge 2: Scheduling Based on Resource Requirements

Now the second part here states that a pod requires certain amount of memory and computing resource to run properly. And this is expected.

So let's assume that app C pod requires 512 MB of Ram and two core CPU to run optimally.

Now, how will you tell Kubernetes that app C pod requires this much amount of Ram and this much amount of CPU? And based on this requirement, Kubernetes should schedule the pod accordingly.

Now, in a very simplistic terms, what you can do is you can go ahead and maybe assign a simple label to the nodes and use the node selector to go ahead and create the pod in the largest node. But this is something that does not really work well specifically when you have thousands of worker nodes.

So you need to be able to clearly state the memory as well as the CPU related requirement to Kubernetes. And Kubernetes can go ahead and look into all of the hundreds or thousands of worker nodes and then schedule the pod accordingly.

## What Are Requests and Limits?

So in order to overcome these two specific challenges that we were discussing, requests and limits proves to be very useful.

So at a high level overview, using the requests and limits, you can control the amount of resource that can be assigned to the pod, and also the appropriate node in which a pod can be launched.

### Request vs Limit Explained

For example, you have a request and you have a limit. Request is more of a guaranteed scenario.

Let's say that your pod really requires 256 MB of Ram to run. If it does not really get this much amount of memory, it will not really work properly. So this is part of a guaranteed memory. So this is what the request is.

Limit is what is the maximum number of memory that a pod can consume. I know that my pod requires Request 256 MB. But what happens if it suddenly starts to use a lot of memory in a node? So in order to control the maximum memory that a pod can use, you make use of limit.

